<h2 class="doc-h2">On a phone</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Below <code>md</code> the shelf gives way to one button. The same links are rendered a second
    time inside it, and each <code>FloatingDockItem</code> learns which copy it is from its dock,
    so nothing is written twice at the call site. <code>DesktopClass</code> and
    <code>MobileClass</code> are upstream's <code>desktopClassName</code> and
    <code>mobileClassName</code> — here they keep the phone layout on at every width.
</p>
<Example Source="FloatingDockMobile.razor"><FloatingDockMobile /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">What a reader without a pointer gets</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The magnification follows a mouse and nothing else, so it is decoration: a keyboard reaches
    every tile, the focus ring is the one every control here wears, and the title appears on focus
    as it does on hover. A tile's name is its <code>Title</code>, which is what a screen reader
    announces. With <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> set the tiles keep their resting size and
    the menu opens without the stagger.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">When not to use it</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    For a handful of destinations that are icons first — an app's sections, a portfolio's links.
    Not for primary navigation that needs words to be understood, where a row of labelled links
    or a <a href="/components/navigation-menu">Navigation Menu</a> says more, and not for more
    than six or seven items: the row grows wider as a tile swells and a long one has nowhere to go.
</p>
